1
00:00:00,480 --> 00:00:05,440
Thandi saved R1,250 on
a TV on Black Friday.

2
00:00:05,520 --> 00:00:10,400
Twelve months later, she'd
paid about R6,570 for it.

3
00:00:10,480 --> 00:00:13,560
That's more than the full
price, before the sale.

4
00:00:13,600 --> 00:00:15,400
She didn't do anything strange.

5
00:00:15,440 --> 00:00:19,680
She did what millions of South Africans
will do on the 27th of November:

6
00:00:19,760 --> 00:00:21,800
she put it on her store card.

7
00:00:21,840 --> 00:00:24,560
If you're planning to buy
anything on credit this month,

8
00:00:24,640 --> 00:00:27,280
this video could save
you hundreds of rand.

9
00:00:27,520 --> 00:00:29,320
So where did the money go?

10
00:00:29,360 --> 00:00:32,920
In this video, you'll see exactly
where every rand of it went,

11
00:00:32,960 --> 00:00:35,560
the costs nobody tells
you about at the till,

12
00:00:35,600 --> 00:00:38,600
and the five traps to
dodge before you swipe.

13
00:00:38,640 --> 00:00:39,760
And stay to the end,

14
00:00:39,840 --> 00:00:43,400
because there's a version of this
deal that costs almost double.

15
00:00:43,440 --> 00:00:44,920
Let's start from zero.

16
00:00:44,960 --> 00:00:47,360
First, what is a store card?

17
00:00:47,440 --> 00:00:51,960
A store card is a loan from the shop,
or from a lender working with the shop.

18
00:00:52,000 --> 00:00:54,747
You get a limit, say R8,000.

19
00:00:54,773 --> 00:00:59,480
You can buy up to that amount now,
and pay it back later, a bit every month.

20
00:00:59,520 --> 00:01:02,053
The law calls this a credit facility.

21
00:01:02,107 --> 00:01:06,200
It works like a credit card, but you
can usually only use it at that shop,

22
00:01:06,240 --> 00:01:07,973
or that group of shops.

23
00:01:08,027 --> 00:01:09,960
Why do shops like them so much?

24
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:13,640
Because with a store card,
the sale doesn't end at the till.

25
00:01:13,680 --> 00:01:17,720
Every month you take to pay,
the lender earns interest and fees.

26
00:01:17,760 --> 00:01:21,440
That's the business. And it's why the
card is offered to you at the till,

27
00:01:21,520 --> 00:01:23,720
right when you're excited about the deal.

28
00:01:23,760 --> 00:01:26,040
And here's the part most people miss.

29
00:01:26,080 --> 00:01:29,320
The price on the shelf
is not the price you pay.

30
00:01:29,360 --> 00:01:33,800
When you pay later, you pay the shelf
price, plus the cost of borrowing.

31
00:01:33,840 --> 00:01:36,080
That cost has three parts.

32
00:01:36,160 --> 00:01:40,120
Part one: interest. Interest is
the price of borrowing money,

33
00:01:40,160 --> 00:01:43,000
as a percentage of what
you owe, every year.

34
00:01:43,040 --> 00:01:46,187
The National Credit Act sets
a maximum for store cards:

35
00:01:46,213 --> 00:01:49,560
the Reserve Bank's repo rate plus 14%.

36
00:01:49,600 --> 00:01:52,920
Right now the repo rate is 7.25%.

37
00:01:52,960 --> 00:01:56,853
So the maximum is 21.25% a year.

38
00:01:56,907 --> 00:02:01,653
To feel that: owe R5,000 for a
whole year without paying anything,

39
00:02:01,707 --> 00:02:05,440
and the interest alone
is more than R1,060.

40
00:02:05,520 --> 00:02:09,160
Just for waiting. And because
the cap is tied to the repo rate,

41
00:02:09,200 --> 00:02:11,560
it moves when the Reserve Bank moves.

42
00:02:11,600 --> 00:02:16,587
The next decision is on the 19th of
November, eight days before Black Friday.

43
00:02:16,693 --> 00:02:19,280
Part two: the monthly service fee.

44
00:02:19,360 --> 00:02:22,440
Many store cards charge you
just for having the account.

45
00:02:22,480 --> 00:02:26,520
The legal maximum is R69
a month, including VAT.

46
00:02:26,560 --> 00:02:30,040
At the maximum, that's R828 a year.

47
00:02:30,080 --> 00:02:32,680
Part three: credit life insurance.

48
00:02:32,720 --> 00:02:37,920
It covers what you owe if you die,
become disabled, or lose your job.

49
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:42,800
The cap is R4.50 a month
for every R1,000 you owe.

50
00:02:42,880 --> 00:02:47,120
It can be useful. But it's charged
every month on what you owe,

51
00:02:47,200 --> 00:02:51,680
so the more you owe, and the longer
you owe it, the more you pay.

52
00:02:51,760 --> 00:02:56,960
And if you open a brand new account, there
can also be a once-off initiation fee:

53
00:02:57,040 --> 00:03:02,880
up to R1,207.50.
Small numbers, one by one.

54
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:04,800
Now watch what they do together.

55
00:03:05,668 --> 00:03:11,508
Back to Thandi. The TV
normally costs R6,250.

56
00:03:11,588 --> 00:03:14,548
On Black Friday, it's R5,000.

57
00:03:14,628 --> 00:03:20,468
That's 20% off. She "saves" R1,250.

58
00:03:20,548 --> 00:03:23,988
She already has a store card,
so she puts it on the card,

59
00:03:24,068 --> 00:03:26,468
and pays it off over twelve months.

60
00:03:26,548 --> 00:03:30,148
Let's say her card charges
the maximum the law allows.

61
00:03:30,228 --> 00:03:35,268
Her instalment, with interest,
is about R466 a month.

62
00:03:35,348 --> 00:03:39,348
Add the R69 service fee,
and the credit life insurance,

63
00:03:39,388 --> 00:03:42,868
and she pays about R548 a month.

64
00:03:43,028 --> 00:03:47,748
After twelve months,
she's paid about R6,570.

65
00:03:47,828 --> 00:03:52,548
That's about R1,570 more
than the sale price.

66
00:03:52,668 --> 00:03:56,308
Her R1,250 discount is gone,

67
00:03:56,348 --> 00:03:59,561
and she's paid more than
the full price on the shelf.

68
00:03:59,615 --> 00:04:02,468
And remember that version
that costs almost double?

69
00:04:02,548 --> 00:04:06,708
Here it is. The salesperson says:
"Make it thirty-six months,

70
00:04:06,748 --> 00:04:11,508
and it's only about R271 a
month." It sounds cheaper.

71
00:04:11,628 --> 00:04:14,948
It isn't. Over thirty-six
months, the interest,

72
00:04:15,028 --> 00:04:17,868
the fees and the insurance keep running.

73
00:04:17,908 --> 00:04:21,881
Thandi would pay almost R9,750.

74
00:04:21,935 --> 00:04:26,121
For a R5,000 TV. Nearly two TVs,

75
00:04:26,175 --> 00:04:30,148
and she'd still be paying for it when
the next two Black Fridays come round.

76
00:04:30,228 --> 00:04:32,628
Here's why. On thirty-six months,

77
00:04:32,708 --> 00:04:38,201
the R69 fee alone adds up to R2,484.

78
00:04:38,255 --> 00:04:42,108
That's half the TV again,
before a cent of interest.

79
00:04:42,148 --> 00:04:44,028
Let's put them side by side.

80
00:04:44,068 --> 00:04:47,188
Cash: R5,000. Twelve months:

81
00:04:47,268 --> 00:04:51,468
about R6,570. Thirty-six months:

82
00:04:51,508 --> 00:04:55,828
almost R9,750. Same TV.

83
00:04:55,948 --> 00:04:58,428
Same shop. Same Black Friday.

84
00:04:58,468 --> 00:05:02,428
Your card may charge less than the
maximum, so check your own statement.

85
00:05:02,468 --> 00:05:07,468
But the pattern is always the same:
the longer you take, the more you pay.

86
00:05:07,508 --> 00:05:11,068
So here are the five traps to
watch for this Black Friday.

87
00:05:11,108 --> 00:05:15,295
Number five is the one that
keeps people paying for years.

88
00:05:15,401 --> 00:05:18,575
Trap one: only looking
at the monthly amount.

89
00:05:18,601 --> 00:05:22,468
"Only R271 a month" hides the total.

90
00:05:22,548 --> 00:05:25,708
Always ask: what will I pay in total?

91
00:05:25,748 --> 00:05:28,108
Trap two: the fake discount.

92
00:05:28,148 --> 00:05:32,268
A "was" price isn't always
what the item really sold for.

93
00:05:32,308 --> 00:05:34,508
Check what it cost a few weeks ago.

94
00:05:34,548 --> 00:05:36,748
Trap three: a higher limit.

95
00:05:36,788 --> 00:05:39,721
Around sales, you may
be offered more credit.

96
00:05:39,775 --> 00:05:41,775
A bigger limit isn't more money.

97
00:05:41,801 --> 00:05:44,028
It's more debt you're allowed to take.

98
00:05:44,068 --> 00:05:47,228
Trap four: the insurance
you didn't choose.

99
00:05:47,268 --> 00:05:50,201
The law says you can use
your own policy instead,

100
00:05:50,255 --> 00:05:52,268
as long as it gives the same cover.

101
00:05:52,308 --> 00:05:54,948
If you already have cover, ask.

102
00:05:55,188 --> 00:05:58,228
Trap five: paying only the minimum.

103
00:05:58,468 --> 00:06:00,588
The minimum keeps the account open.

104
00:06:00,788 --> 00:06:02,428
It doesn't clear the debt.

105
00:06:02,708 --> 00:06:05,828
Interest keeps running on
everything you still owe.

106
00:06:06,704 --> 00:06:10,064
Here's how to shop this Black
Friday without the trap.

107
00:06:10,304 --> 00:06:13,424
Step one: make a list before the day.

108
00:06:13,544 --> 00:06:17,104
Write down what you actually need,
and the most you'll spend.

109
00:06:17,224 --> 00:06:19,824
Step two: check the real price.

110
00:06:19,904 --> 00:06:24,464
Look at the price a few weeks before the
sale, and compare two or three shops.

111
00:06:24,544 --> 00:06:27,264
Step three: pay cash if you can.

112
00:06:27,384 --> 00:06:29,744
Even better, start saving now.

113
00:06:29,824 --> 00:06:33,984
If Thandi had put away R548
a month for ten months,

114
00:06:34,064 --> 00:06:38,784
she'd have R5,480.
Enough for the TV in cash,

115
00:06:38,864 --> 00:06:41,824
with money to spare,
and no interest at all.

116
00:06:41,904 --> 00:06:45,024
That's the Black Friday
deal nobody advertises.

117
00:06:45,184 --> 00:06:47,664
Step four: if you must use credit,

118
00:06:47,784 --> 00:06:52,704
ask for the full cost in writing before
you sign: the instalment, the fees,

119
00:06:52,744 --> 00:06:54,864
the insurance, and the total.

120
00:06:55,024 --> 00:06:58,224
Step five: choose the
shortest term you can afford,

121
00:06:58,304 --> 00:07:00,784
and pay more than the minimum every month.

122
00:07:00,824 --> 00:07:04,224
And if there's an interest-free
offer, read the terms carefully,

123
00:07:04,264 --> 00:07:06,784
and ask what happens if
you don't clear it in time.

124
00:07:07,024 --> 00:07:08,704
Put a reminder in your phone.

125
00:07:08,744 --> 00:07:12,864
And if your store cards are already more
than you can handle, don't add to them.

126
00:07:13,024 --> 00:07:16,704
Speak to the lender early,
or to a registered debt counsellor.

127
00:07:16,744 --> 00:07:19,584
The National Credit Regulator
lists them on its website.

128
00:07:19,904 --> 00:07:22,704
Let's recap. A store card is a loan.

129
00:07:23,024 --> 00:07:27,664
What you pay is the price, plus
interest, plus fees, plus insurance.

130
00:07:27,984 --> 00:07:32,704
The legal maximums today:
21.25% interest a year,

131
00:07:32,824 --> 00:07:35,264
R69 a month in service fees,

132
00:07:35,344 --> 00:07:39,904
and R4.50 a month per R1,000
for credit life insurance.

133
00:07:39,984 --> 00:07:45,904
For Thandi, a R5,000 TV became
about R6,570 over twelve

134
00:07:46,064 --> 00:07:50,624
months, and almost R9,750 over thirty-six.

135
00:07:50,664 --> 00:07:55,184
Thandi's lesson is simple: the real
price is what you've paid at the end,

136
00:07:55,264 --> 00:07:56,784
not the number on the shelf.

137
00:07:56,944 --> 00:08:02,224
So before you swipe, ask one question:
what will this cost me in total?

138
00:08:02,304 --> 00:08:05,824
This video is general information,
not financial advice.

139
00:08:05,944 --> 00:08:10,064
Your card's rates and fees may be
different, so check your own statement.

140
00:08:10,144 --> 00:08:12,784
If this helped,
subscribe to Money From Zero.

141
00:08:12,864 --> 00:08:16,464
New Shorts every day, and a
full guide like this every week.

